Tor is a connection-based low-latency anonymous communication system that
protects TCP streams: web browsing, instant messaging, irc, ssh, etc.

On OpenBSD, the packaged default torrc enables Sandbox 1, which applies
unveil(2) and pledge(2). The process running Tor does not receive pledge's
exec or error promise. Managed pluggable transports (ClientTransportPlugin
or ServerTransportPlugin with exec) are therefore rejected by Tor while
Sandbox is enabled. External SOCKS4/SOCKS5 client proxies and external server
proxies do not require Tor to execute a program and remain supported under
the sandbox profile when their endpoints are accessible.

The rc.d(8) script starts tor as the unprivileged _tor user through
daemon_user and lets rc.subr handle backgrounding. The packaged torrc does
not set User or RunAsDaemon. Do not combine daemon_user with tor's User
setting: the second privilege drop cannot call setgroups(2). Binding a
listener below port 1024 requires a privileged wrapper and a separately
reviewed service configuration.
